00:16:53.820 --> 00:16:54.520
<v Maya>engagement by 22%.</v>

161
00:16:56.067 --> 00:17:05.429
<v Daniel>Wait, hold on. You just told me Beckerman explicitly tested for that exact narrowing of topics, this AI paradox, in his data set and found absolutely no sign of it.</v>

162
00:17:05.649 --> 00:17:06.269
<v Maya>I did, yes.</v>

163
00:17:06.449 --> 00:17:12.890
<v Daniel>So who is wrong here? Is science getting faster but narrower, or is it maintaining structural continuity?</v>

164
00:17:13.231 --> 00:17:19.252
<v Maya>It's not that one is wrong. It's that they are measuring the concept of a topic in fundamentally different ways.</v>

165
00:17:19.532 --> 00:17:20.292
<v Daniel>Okay, how so?</v>

166
00:17:20.812 --> 00:17:32.445
<v Maya>Howe looked at 41 million papers and used vector embeddings to measure the diameter of knowledge space. Beckerman looked at 2.5 million full-text papers using Jensen-Shannon distance on word distributions.</v>

167
00:17:33.326 --> 00:17:39.033
<v Daniel>You're killing me with the math terms today. Help me visualize how those two methods could produce totally opposite results.</v>

168
00:17:39.275 --> 00:17:42.056
<v Maya>Fair enough. Think of scientific knowledge as a massive forest.</v>

169
00:17:42.156 --> 00:17:42.836
<v Daniel>Okay.</v>

170
00:17:42.856 --> 00:17:57.661
<v Maya>How is using vector embeddings, which is like measuring the physical distance between the tree trunks. By that metric, he says the forest is getting denser and narrower, like everyone is clustering around the same types of trees. Got it. Beckerman is using Jensen-Shannon distance, which is like</v>

171
00:17:57.841 --> 00:18:07.865
<v Maya>counting the variety of leaves on the ground. And by his metric, the variety of leaves hasn't changed at all. They are both measuring the forest, but getting different results because their definitions are different.</v>

172
00:18:08.005 --> 00:18:11.568
<v Daniel>So you're saying how measures the trunks and Beckerman measures the leaves?</v>

173
00:18:12.008 --> 00:18:16.791
<v Maya>Exactly. It just highlights how difficult it is to get a clear macro level signal right now.</v>

174
00:18:17.392 --> 00:18:24.116
<v Daniel>OK, so the AI paradox might just be a mirage depending on how you look at the data. But do any of our background sources actually agree with our new leads?</v>

175
00:18:24.436 --> 00:18:34.624
<v Maya>Yes, in some very specific validating ways. Take the 2025 science paper by Kusumegi and team. They found that LLM adopters raised their paper output by anywhere from 23.7 to 89.3 percent.</v>

176
00:18:37.118 --> 00:18:40.719
<v Daniel>Which beautifully supports the massive productivity claims in the OpenAI study.</v>

177
00:18:41.039 --> 00:18:46.681
<v Maya>Exactly. It proves the speedup isn't just in the chat window. It's resulting in actual submitted manuscripts.</v>

178
00:18:47.121 --> 00:19:00.445
<v Daniel>Though it's worth noting Kusumegi also warned that LLMs reverse the link between writing complexity and paper quality. Like, the AI makes it so easy to write beautifully that a well-written paper no longer guarantees the underlying science is actually sound.</v>

179
00:19:00.787 --> 00:19:15.716
<v Maya>Which is an absolute nightmare for peer reviewers. And finally, there is the 2025 Nature Human Behavior paper by Liying. They tracked LLM-modified content and found it rose fastest in computer science up to 17.5% and rose the least</v>

180
00:19:15.796 --> 00:19:16.456
<v Maya>in mathematics.</v>

181
00:19:17.176 --> 00:19:28.423
<v Daniel>Oh, which perfectly validates the exact same field-by-field vocabulary ordering that Beckerman just found in the NJIT study. Computer science showed the strongest vocabulary shift and math showed almost none.</v>

182
00:19:28.812 --> 00:19:35.737
<v Maya>Exactly. So the macro level indicators are internally consistent, even if they aren't showing a massive structural break yet.</v>

183
00:19:36.217 --> 00:19:47.044
<v Daniel>When you synthesize all of this, a cohesive picture really emerges. The micro level changes inside the chat windows are profound, highly concentrated, and they are speeding up cognitive tasks.</v>

184
00:19:47.865 --> 00:19:48.485
<v Maya>Very much so.</v>

185
00:19:48.766 --> 00:19:58.292
<v Daniel>But the macro level changes are real in terms of vocabulary and output volume, but they just haven't yet disrupted the fundamental structures or topic diversity of science.</v>

186
00:19:58.647 --> 00:20:06.331
<v Maya>Right. And for the scientists, policymakers, and university administrators listening right now, the immediate implication seems pretty clear.</v>

187
00:20:06.811 --> 00:20:21.219
<v Daniel>Yeah, your policy doesn't need to panic about AI slop destroying the scientific record. The data says that isn't happening. Instead, your policy needs to focus entirely on managing adoption and fixing those physical and financial bottlenecks.</v>

188
00:20:21.999 --> 00:20:26.902
<v Daniel>Because the cognitive bottlenecks, you know, the reading, the writing, the coding, those are already disappearing.</v>

189
00:20:27.255 --> 00:20:41.845
<v Maya>The human mind is being freed up from the friction of execution. Over the next few years, what will really settle this debate is whether the massive reading and coding speedups from the OpenAI study can eventually generate enough pressure to break through the funding and staffing</v>

190
00:20:41.865 --> 00:20:42.346
<v Maya>bottlenecks.</v>

191
00:20:42.906 --> 00:20:45.128
<v Daniel>Will faster coding lead to cheaper experiments?</v>

192
00:20:45.948 --> 00:20:55.715
<v Maya>Will AI peer review agents speed up publication? If those physical traffic jams clear up, that is when we will see if the structural continuity Beckerman documented finally shifts.</v>

193
00:20:56.229 --> 00:21:00.450
<v Daniel>Which leaves you with one final slightly provocative thought to mull over today.</v>

194
00:21:00.971 --> 00:21:01.491
<v Maya>Oh, I'm ready.</v>

195
00:21:02.231 --> 00:21:14.375
<v Daniel>If AI is fundamentally accelerating the thinking and writing parts of science to near instant speeds, but the doing part, running the physical experiments, navigating the peer review, securing the grant money, remains painfully slow.</v>

196
00:21:14.715 --> 00:21:14.935
<v Maya>Right.</v>

197
00:21:15.495 --> 00:21:30.269
<v Daniel>Will the defining skill of a future Nobel laureate simply be their ability to manage real world traffic jams rather than their ability to generate novel ideas? because it seems the engine on our train has already been upgraded. The only thing left to do is see who can clear the tracks.</v>
